    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with **Jackson Merrill** (75.10) and **Gunnar Henderson** (72.48) headlining as our clear offensive anchors. **Jackson Merrill** benefits significantly from a favorable matchup against McGreevy, where his +13% platoon adjustment and lead-off spot maximize his potential for both counting stats and stolen bases. While **Gunnar Henderson** faces a slight negative adjustment due to the platoon split against Lopez, his elite park factors and top-of-the-order positioning keep him firmly in the \"Strong\" start tier. **Coby Mayo** (66.72) also presents a high-upside play; despite the platoon hurdle, the combination of his power profile and a advantageous venue allows him to significantly outperform his base projection.\n\nOur most difficult decision came at the margins of the utility and bench spots, where **Nolan Schanuel** (48.70) narrowly missed the final cut. While his projected efficiency is respectable, he ultimately lost out to the higher-floor profiles of **Miguel Vargas** and **Sam Antonacci**. **Sam Antonacci** (56.74) remains a priority start today, largely driven by an elite xwOBA and a prime lead-off opportunity against Hancock. Conversely, we are keeping a close eye on **Kyle Tucker** (52.14); facing an elite arm like Sale is a distinct warning flag, though his talent remains too high to bench in a 5x5 format despite the tempered expectations.\n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the lineup fluidity today. Both **Drake Baldwin** and **Heliot Ramos** are currently carrying \"Lineup Pending\" designations; should either be dropped down the order or benched by their respective managers, their efficiency scores will crater. Furthermore, while **Josh Lowe** (53.48) is slotted in, he faces a tough assignment against Cease. However, the decision to play him is heavily influenced by the rare synergy of his 14% platoon edge and the fact that Cease\u2019s slow delivery offers a prime opportunity for **Josh Lowe** to hunt for a stolen base, a critical priority for the Waves given our heavy weighting of speed categories.",
